In an adult skeleton,

317
00:13:00,910 --> 00:13:03,430
highline cartilage is
located in synovial

318
00:13:03,430 --> 00:13:05,690
joints, costal
cartilages, and the

319
00:13:05,690 --> 00:13:07,990
cartilages in the pharynx
and bronchial tree.

320
00:13:08,270 --> 00:13:10,490
Elastic cartilages,
shown in

321
00:13:10,490 --> 00:13:12,490
green, are the
flimsy cartilages,

322
00:13:12,490 --> 00:13:14,250
such as the
oracle of the ear.

323
00:13:14,510 --> 00:13:17,110
And finally,
fibrocartilages, which

324
00:13:17,110 --> 00:13:19,510
are in red, form the
vertebral discs in

325
00:13:19,510 --> 00:13:21,430
the spine and also
some other specialized

326
00:13:21,430 --> 00:13:23,430
cartilages, such
as the menisci

327
00:13:23,430 --> 00:13:25,970
in the knee and the
***** symphysis.

328
00:13:27,450 --> 00:13:30,450
As you study
musculoskeletal anatomy,

329
00:13:30,450 --> 00:13:32,690
joints are obviously
a focal point.

330
00:13:32,930 --> 00:13:34,950
Joints are areas
where musculoskeletal

331
00:13:34,950 --> 00:13:36,750
structures are
joined in complex

332
00:13:36,750 --> 00:13:39,570
ways to permit and
restrict motion.

333
00:13:39,570 --> 00:13:41,910
Joint injuries and
joint degeneration

334
00:13:41,910 --> 00:13:43,970
are significant
concerns in medicine.

335
00:13:44,270 --> 00:13:46,590
Joints consist of many
parts, including the

336
00:13:46,590 --> 00:13:48,650
proximal and distal
ends of the bones,

337
00:13:48,650 --> 00:13:52,170
the articular cartilages,
and other cartilaginous

338
00:13:52,170 --> 00:13:54,030
structures in the
joints, such as

339
00:13:54,030 --> 00:13:56,550
the fibrocartilaginous
menisci in the knee.

340
00:13:56,770 --> 00:13:58,910
Joints are held together
by ligaments, as

341
00:13:58,910 --> 00:14:01,310
you can see in the
top right figure, but

342
00:14:01,310 --> 00:14:03,090
also by muscles that
cross these joints,

343
00:14:03,090 --> 00:14:05,510
which are not pictured
here. The strength,

344
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:07,670
mobility, and motions
permitted by a

345
00:14:07,670 --> 00:14:10,250
joint are determined
by several variables.

346
00:14:10,550 --> 00:14:12,850
These include the
shapes of the articular

347
00:14:12,850 --> 00:14:15,350
surfaces, which determine
which directions

348
00:14:15,350 --> 00:14:17,510
the joint can
move. The ligaments

349
00:14:17,510 --> 00:14:19,450
determine how much a
joint can bend in some

350
00:14:19,450 --> 00:14:21,870
directions and prevent
excess movements

351
00:14:21,870 --> 00:14:24,450
beyond that range. And
in other directions,

352
00:14:24,450 --> 00:14:26,230
ligaments completely
prevent movement.

353
00:14:26,680 --> 00:14:28,890
Muscles move the
joints, but they also

354
00:14:28,890 --> 00:14:31,390
act as dynamic ligaments
and also determine

355
00:14:31,390 --> 00:14:33,590
how much a joint
can bend and prevent

356
00:14:33,590 --> 00:14:35,590
excess movements
beyond that range.

357
00:14:36,290 --> 00:14:38,810
Joints have complex
vasculature.

358
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:41,610
Anastomoses, which
you will study in

359
00:14:41,610 --> 00:14:43,850
detail later, are
basically redundant

360
00:14:43,850 --> 00:14:46,050
blood supplies. The
same Joint tissues can

361
00:14:46,050 --> 00:14:48,130
be supplied by numerous
arteries, which

362
00:14:48,130 --> 00:14:49,750
assure that blood
moves to the joint

363
00:14:49,750 --> 00:14:52,390
and to the tissues
beyond the joint, no

364
00:14:52,390 --> 00:14:54,310
matter what position
the joint is in.

365
00:14:54,450 --> 00:14:57,610
Basically anastomoses
around joints prevent

366
00:14:57,610 --> 00:15:00,190
a highly flexed joint
from cutting off

367
00:15:00,190 --> 00:15:03,230
blood supply to itself
and distal body parts.

368
00:15:03,410 --> 00:15:05,770
Finally, joint
structures are richly

369
00:15:05,770 --> 00:15:07,250
innervated.
The tendons and

370
00:15:07,250 --> 00:15:08,870
ligaments are highly
sensitive to the

371
00:15:08,870 --> 00:15:10,610
tension being
exerted upon them.

372
00:15:10,620 --> 00:15:12,350
This contributes
to something

373
00:15:12,350 --> 00:15:14,370
called proprioception,
which is the

374
00:15:14,370 --> 00:15:16,270
ability of a person
to sense what

375
00:15:16,270 --> 00:15:18,130
position their
body parts are in.

376
00:15:18,390 --> 00:15:20,390
Proprioception is an
important sense as it

377
00:15:20,390 --> 00:15:22,670
contributes to motor
abilities, such as

378
00:15:22,670 --> 00:15:25,330
touching your nose with
your eyes, typing without

379
00:15:25,330 --> 00:15:27,310
looking at your fingers,
or walking up and

380
00:15:27,310 --> 00:15:29,150
down stairs without
looking at your feet.

381
00:15:29,230 --> 00:15:31,410
There's an easy
shortcut to understand

382
00:15:31,410 --> 00:15:32,950
which nerves
innervate which

383
00:15:32,950 --> 00:15:35,050
joints. It's known
as Hilton's Law.

384
00:15:37,290 --> 00:15:40,410
John Hilton is a famous
British surgeon who

385
00:15:40,410 --> 00:15:43,190
first noted what we
now call Hilton's Law.

386
00:15:43,230 --> 00:15:45,810
Hilton's Law says that
the nerve supplying

387
00:15:45,810 --> 00:15:48,010
the muscles extending
directly across

388
00:15:48,010 --> 00:15:50,850
and acting at a
given joint not only

389
00:15:50,850 --> 00:15:53,230
supplies the muscle,
but also innervates the

390
00:15:53,230 --> 00:15:55,970
joint and the skin
overlying the muscle.

391
00:15:56,230 --> 00:15:58,270
Since you will be
studying the attachments,

392
00:15:58,270 --> 00:16:00,050
innervations,
and actions of

393
00:16:00,050 --> 00:16:02,090
muscles, you will
be able to use that

394
00:16:02,090 --> 00:16:04,030
knowledge to infer
which nerves contribute

395
00:16:04,030 --> 00:16:06,130
to the innervation
of associated joint

396
00:16:06,130 --> 00:16:08,670
structures. So, no
need to memorize.

397
00:16:11,930 --> 00:16:13,710
There are three types
of joints in the

398
00:16:13,710 --> 00:16:15,910
body. We have already
discussed synovial

399
00:16:15,910 --> 00:16:17,870
joints a little. They
are the most common

400
00:16:17,870 --> 00:16:19,570
joint in the body and
are capable of the

401
00:16:19,570 --> 00:16:22,030
most movement and the
most diverse kinds of

402
00:16:22,030 --> 00:16:24,590
movement. All synovial
joints are enclosed

403
00:16:24,590 --> 00:16:27,350
in a synovial capsule,
which is filled

404
00:16:27,350 --> 00:16:29,300
with a lubricant
called synovial fluid,

405
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:31,670
and the articular
surfaces of the bones

406
00:16:31,670 --> 00:16:34,230
are capped with
articular cartilages. We

407
00:16:34,230 --> 00:16:36,370
will study synovial
joints in more detail.

408
00:16:36,370 --> 00:16:38,430
On the upper right,
fibrous joints

409
00:16:38,430 --> 00:16:39,990
are the sutures
in the skull.

410
00:16:39,990 --> 00:16:42,490
These are immobile
or minimally mobile.

411
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:45,550
Syndysmosis are a
type of fibrous joint

412
00:16:45,550 --> 00:16:47,330
that has the potential
for more motion.

413
00:16:47,330 --> 00:16:49,450
The example here
is the interosseous

414
00:16:49,450 --> 00:16:51,490
membrane between the
ulna and radius in

415
00:16:51,490 --> 00:16:53,650
the forearm, which
allows the radius to

416
00:16:53,650 --> 00:16:56,130
rotate about the
ulna. The lower right

417
00:16:56,130 --> 00:16:58,570
shows two types of
cartilaginous joints.

418
00:16:58,850 --> 00:17:00,930
Primary cartilaginous
joints, which

419
00:17:00,930 --> 00:17:02,880
are also called
synchondroses,

420
00:17:03,350 --> 00:17:05,910
are the joints
between the diaphyses

421
00:17:05,910 --> 00:17:07,730
and the epiphyses
of long bones.

422
00:17:07,730 --> 00:17:10,630
Primary cartilaginous
joints are not mobile.

423
00:17:10,630 --> 00:17:13,190
These bones fuse
with age, and primary

424
00:17:13,190 --> 00:17:15,870
cartilaginous joints are
not present in adults.

425
00:17:16,470 --> 00:17:19,090
On the other hand,
secondary cartilaginous

426
00:17:19,090 --> 00:17:21,350
joints do persist in
adults. The most common

427
00:17:21,350 --> 00:17:24,550
examples of cartilaginous
joints are the

428
00:17:24,550 --> 00:17:26,910
vertebral discs, which
allow limited motion.

429
00:17:28,350 --> 00:17:30,650
You will receive an
entire lecture on the

430
00:17:30,650 --> 00:17:33,070
anatomy of synovial
joints, so we will not

431
00:17:33,070 --> 00:17:35,030
cover that here.
However, you should be

432
00:17:35,030 --> 00:17:37,170
familiar with the basic
components of a synovial

433
00:17:37,170 --> 00:17:39,750
joint, including the
joint capsule, which

434
00:17:39,750 --> 00:17:41,830
surrounds and seals
the synovial capsule,

435
00:17:42,890 --> 00:17:45,750
the synovial membrane,
which secretes

436
00:17:45,750 --> 00:17:48,570
synovial fluid, and the
articular cartilage on

437
00:17:48,570 --> 00:17:50,590
the proximal and distal
ends of the bone.

438
00:17:53,390 --> 00:17:55,510
And now let's move
on to joint motion.

439
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:57,330
If you're not familiar
with the different

440
00:17:57,330 --> 00:17:59,210
types of motions,
please review them

441
00:17:59,210 --> 00:18:01,330
using this figure or
some other resources.

442
00:18:01,710 --> 00:18:04,330
One helpful aspect of
the musculoskeletal

443
00:18:04,330 --> 00:18:06,330
system is that you can
use your own body as

444
00:18:06,330 --> 00:18:08,410
a cheat sheet to help
you study and understand

445
00:18:08,410 --> 00:18:10,690
the motions of each
joint. There is no

446
00:18:10,690 --> 00:18:12,950
harm in moving a joint
or palpating part of

447
00:18:12,950 --> 00:18:14,950
your musculoskeletal
system during a test.

448
00:18:14,970 --> 00:18:17,350
Most of the
musculoskeletal system is

449
00:18:17,350 --> 00:18:20,110
observable through
its motions, surface

450
00:18:20,110 --> 00:18:22,770
anatomy, and palpation.
As you study the

451
00:18:22,770 --> 00:18:25,150
musculoskeletal system,
study the anatomy

452
00:18:25,150 --> 00:18:26,970
of your own body
through these means

453
00:18:26,970 --> 00:18:28,790
because with practice,
it will become your

454
00:18:28,790 --> 00:18:31,110
most useful study
guide and reference.

455
00:18:33,590 --> 00:18:35,790
You are naturally
already familiar with

456
00:18:35,790 --> 00:18:37,770
the flexion and
extension capabilities

457
00:18:37,770 --> 00:18:40,190
of most joints, but
there are aspects of

458
00:18:40,190 --> 00:18:41,610
motion that you
may not have ever

459
00:18:41,610 --> 00:18:44,430
recognized. As an
example, try and evaluate

460
00:18:44,430 --> 00:18:46,850
