rotate about an axis.

486
00:19:51,770 --> 00:19:53,650
Again, you can
discover the motions

487
00:19:53,650 --> 00:19:55,550
of joints by
examining yourself.

488
00:19:55,710 --> 00:19:56,910
No need to memorize.

489
00:19:56,970 --> 00:19:59,350
Try to deduce
what motions the

490
00:19:59,770 --> 00:20:02,290
metacarpophalangeal
joints are capable of.

491
00:20:07,910 --> 00:20:09,390
In Part 3 of
this lecture, we

492
00:20:09,390 --> 00:20:10,710
will cover muscles
and the different

493
00:20:10,710 --> 00:20:12,330
types of muscle
contractions.

494
00:20:13,150 --> 00:20:15,230
It is pointless to
know the name and

495
00:20:15,230 --> 00:20:16,890
anatomical location
of a muscle

496
00:20:16,890 --> 00:20:19,370
without knowing its
associated detachments

497
00:20:19,370 --> 00:20:21,150
and the primary
action that the

498
00:20:21,150 --> 00:20:22,890
muscle produces
when it contracts.

499
00:20:22,990 --> 00:20:25,430
Secondly, it is important
to have familiarity

500
00:20:25,430 --> 00:20:27,110
with the innervations
of muscles,

501
00:20:27,110 --> 00:20:29,490
as the evaluation of
muscle function and

502
00:20:29,490 --> 00:20:31,570
muscle impairments is
a means of diagnosing

503
00:20:31,570 --> 00:20:33,510
neurological injuries
and disorders.

504
00:20:33,510 --> 00:20:35,290
There are some
convenient shortcuts

505
00:20:35,290 --> 00:20:37,070
to learning the
innervations of muscles

506
00:20:37,070 --> 00:20:38,690
of the limbs,
which will actually

507
00:20:38,690 --> 00:20:40,770
make this quite
easy. We will cover

508
00:20:40,770 --> 00:20:42,570
these shortcuts in
subsequent lectures.

509
00:20:42,570 --> 00:20:44,830
Please note that
a muscle name

510
00:20:44,830 --> 00:20:47,650
includes its bellies
and its tendons.

511
00:20:47,650 --> 00:20:49,590
So when I refer
to, for instance,

512
00:20:49,730 --> 00:20:51,190
the biceps brachii,

513
00:20:51,190 --> 00:20:54,150
I am referring to
its long and short

514
00:20:54,150 --> 00:20:56,950
heads and its proximal
and distal tendons.

515
00:20:56,950 --> 00:20:58,950
You should be able
to recognize and know

516
00:20:58,950 --> 00:21:00,810
the names of the
individual bellies of

517
00:21:00,810 --> 00:21:03,090
major muscles that have
multiple bellies or

518
00:21:03,090 --> 00:21:05,470
heads. For example,
as I mentioned, the

519
00:21:05,470 --> 00:21:07,930
biceps brachi has
two bellies, the long

520
00:21:07,930 --> 00:21:09,910
and short. They have
different attachments

521
00:21:09,910 --> 00:21:12,370
and cause non-identical
sets of actions.

522
00:21:12,530 --> 00:21:14,130
The name of the
muscle sometimes

523
00:21:14,130 --> 00:21:16,310
offers major clues
to some aspect

524
00:21:16,310 --> 00:21:17,970
of its anatomy.
For instance,

525
00:21:18,250 --> 00:21:20,010
the adductor
pollicis reveals that

526
00:21:20,010 --> 00:21:21,890
the muscle adducts
the thumb. This

527
00:21:21,890 --> 00:21:23,070
is a helpful shortcut for

528
00:21:23,070 --> 00:21:25,690
remembering or deducing
muscle actions.

529
00:21:28,090 --> 00:21:30,810
Generally speaking,
the term contraction in

530
00:21:30,810 --> 00:21:34,270
physiology refers to a
shortening or tensing

531
00:21:34,270 --> 00:21:37,310
of an organ, especially
muscles. However,

532
00:21:37,310 --> 00:21:39,630
this is a poor and
erroneous definition in

533
00:21:39,630 --> 00:21:42,410
reference to muscles and
often causes confusion

534
00:21:42,410 --> 00:21:44,830
about what a muscle
contraction really is.

535
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:48,670
When we study muscles
and muscle function,

536
00:21:48,670 --> 00:21:50,790
please strike any
concept of shortening

537
00:21:50,790 --> 00:21:53,530
or decreasing length
from your understanding

538
00:21:53,530 --> 00:21:55,970
of the term
contraction. A muscle

539
00:21:55,970 --> 00:21:58,570
contraction is the
activation of muscle fibers

540
00:21:58,570 --> 00:22:00,750
resulting in the
generation of tension.

541
00:22:00,820 --> 00:22:02,530
Nothing other than
the generation

542
00:22:02,530 --> 00:22:04,250
of tension is
required for

543
00:22:04,250 --> 00:22:06,010
a muscle contraction
to occur.

544
00:22:06,010 --> 00:22:08,310
While we might think
of a muscle contraction

545
00:22:08,310 --> 00:22:10,530
as leading to a
shortening of the muscle,

546
00:22:10,530 --> 00:22:12,990
this is not always
the case. A length

547
00:22:12,990 --> 00:22:16,550
change, either shortening
or elongating, may

548
00:22:16,550 --> 00:22:18,930
occur, but it is not
required during a

549
00:22:18,930 --> 00:22:21,110
muscle contraction.
For example, you may

550
00:22:21,110 --> 00:22:23,930
attempt to lift a weight.
If the weight is too

551
00:22:23,930 --> 00:22:26,070
heavy to lift, a
muscle contraction will

552
00:22:26,070 --> 00:22:28,310
have taken place, but
if no movement has

553
00:22:28,310 --> 00:22:30,690
taken place, the muscle
has not shortened.

554
00:22:34,450 --> 00:22:36,790
Having cleared that
up, let's now look

555
00:22:36,790 --> 00:22:38,850
at what the action
of a muscle is.

556
00:22:38,850 --> 00:22:40,850
When you read
anatomy textbooks,

557
00:22:40,850 --> 00:22:42,830
muscles are described
as having actions.

558
00:22:42,830 --> 00:22:45,110
The action of a muscle
is what happens when

559
00:22:45,110 --> 00:22:47,210
the muscle contracts
without resistance and

560
00:22:47,210 --> 00:22:49,890
it produces a movement.
To use the biceps

561
00:22:49,890 --> 00:22:52,610
brakii example again,
when it contracts without

562
00:22:52,610 --> 00:22:55,650
resistance, it causes
the elbow to flex.

563
00:22:55,730 --> 00:22:58,250
The action of the
triceps brakii,

564
00:22:58,250 --> 00:22:59,550
on the other
hand, when it

565
00:22:59,550 --> 00:23:01,050
contracts with
no resistance,

566
00:23:01,050 --> 00:23:03,130
causes the
elbow to extend.

567
00:23:03,290 --> 00:23:05,810
However, in real
life, muscles do not

568
00:23:05,810 --> 00:23:08,310
work in isolation and
without resistance.

569
00:23:08,310 --> 00:23:11,490
The biceps and the
triceps are antagonists.

570
00:23:11,490 --> 00:23:13,730
In other words, they
move the elbow in the

571
00:23:13,730 --> 00:23:16,090
opposite directions
and if they contract at

572
00:23:16,090 --> 00:23:18,670
the same time will work
against one another.

573
00:23:18,770 --> 00:23:21,970
There are also external
forces that muscles

574
00:23:21,970 --> 00:23:25,050
must both combat and
cooperate with, such

575
00:23:25,050 --> 00:23:27,210
as the force of gravity
pulling on the body,

576
00:23:27,590 --> 00:23:29,750
gravity pulling on
other objects that

577
00:23:29,750 --> 00:23:31,570
someone may be
attempting to interact

578
00:23:31,570 --> 00:23:33,390
with, or the
resistance of another

579
00:23:33,390 --> 00:23:35,390
person, such as a
wrestling opponent.

580
00:23:35,850 --> 00:23:37,230
Consequently,
as you will see,

581
00:23:37,230 --> 00:23:38,730
muscles do not
always shorten when

582
00:23:38,730 --> 00:23:41,190
they contract, and
they do not always

583
00:23:41,190 --> 00:23:43,070
produce their
textbook actions.

584
00:23:44,790 --> 00:23:47,410
Walking down stairs is
a classic example of

585
00:23:47,410 --> 00:23:49,850
muscles counteracting
gravity using eccentric

586
00:23:49,850 --> 00:23:51,950
contractions of the
quadriceps muscles.

587
00:23:54,130 --> 00:23:56,830
So at this point,
let's go over the major

588
00:23:56,830 --> 00:23:59,110
types of muscle
contractions. On the

589
00:23:59,110 --> 00:24:01,650
left, Arnold Schwarzenegger
is just standing

590
00:24:01,650 --> 00:24:04,150
there, not moving,
but his muscles are

591
00:24:04,150 --> 00:24:06,950
in a state of tonus.
Tonus is the condition

592
00:24:06,950 --> 00:24:09,880
of your muscles
constantly being in an

593
00:24:09,880 --> 00:24:12,330
involuntary state of
minor contraction.

594
00:24:12,580 --> 00:24:15,170
Tonus is involuntary
and always occurs,

595
00:24:15,170 --> 00:24:17,350
although it diminishes
during REM sleep.

596
00:24:17,350 --> 00:24:18,850
We will not discuss tonic

597
00:24:18,850 --> 00:24:19,890
contractions further.

598
00:24:20,260 --> 00:24:22,590
In the middle
photo, Arnold is

599
00:24:22,590 --> 00:24:24,790
demonstrating an
isometric contraction.

600
00:24:24,790 --> 00:24:27,090
The flexors of his
elbow, including his

601
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:30,070
biceps brachii, and the
extensor, the triceps

602
00:24:30,070 --> 00:24:32,150
brachii, are contracting
in equilibrium

603
00:24:32,150 --> 00:24:34,490
and holding his elbow
in an unmoving state

604
00:24:34,490 --> 00:24:36,970
of stasis. As long
as his elbow is in

605
00:24:36,970 --> 00:24:39,930
the same position, the
muscles do not change

606
00:24:39,930 --> 00:24:41,990
length, although they
are still contracting,

607
00:24:41,990 --> 00:24:44,130
so during an
isometric contraction,

608
00:24:44,130 --> 00:24:46,470
the length of the
muscles do not change.

609
00:24:46,490 --> 00:24:48,350
In the right
photo, Arnold is

610
00:24:48,350 --> 00:24:49,790
working out with
a weight machine.

611
00:24:49,790 --> 00:24:51,670
His elbow is going
through cycles

612
00:24:51,670 --> 00:24:53,930
of extending and
flexing, and the

613
00:24:53,930 --> 00:24:55,950
extensor muscles
in his arm are

614
00:24:55,950 --> 00:24:57,990
changing length with
each contraction.

615
00:24:59,570 --> 00:25:01,950
These are isotonic
contractions.

616
00:25:02,650 --> 00:25:04,390
There are
further two types

617
00:25:04,390 --> 00:25:05,910
of isotonic contractions.

618
00:25:05,910 --> 00:25:08,490
Concentric contractions
are where the

619
00:25:08,490 --> 00:25:11,610
muscles shorten, and
eccentric contractions

620
00:25:11,610 --> 00:25:13,400
are where the
muscle elongates.

621
00:25:13,710 --> 00:25:15,990
It sounds highly
counterintuitive that

622
00:25:15,990 --> 00:25:17,970
a muscle can elongate
while it contracts

623
00:25:17,970 --> 00:25:19,690
because a muscle
can only pull in

624
00:25:19,690 --> 00:25:22,230
one direction. It
cannot push. But I

625
00:25:22,230 --> 00:25:24,230
hope to explain this
in further slides.

626
00:25:25,770 --> 00:25:28,690
In this scenario,
a weightlifter is

627
00:25:28,690 --> 00:25:30,530
lifting and lowering
a weight. weight.

628
00:25:30,530 --> 00:25:33,350
This exercise involves
repeated flexion

629
00:25:33,350 --> 00:25:35,590
and extension of
the elbow against

630
00:25:35,590 --> 00:25:38,190
a constant extrinsic
force, in this

631
00:25:38,190 --> 00:25:40,090
case the pull of
gravity on the weight,

632
00:25:40,210 --> 00:25:41,790
which continually pulls

633
00:25:41,790 --> 00:25:43,730
downward a
constant amount.

634
00:25:43,930 --> 00:25:46,530
Even though the elbow
is both flexing and

635
00:25:46,530 --> 00:25:48,950
extending in this
exercise, bodybuilders

636
00:25:48,950 --> 00:25:50,930
are well aware that
the exercise works

637
00:25:50,930 --> 00:25:53,170
the biceps brachi
but does absolutely

638
00:25:53,170 --> 00:25:55,950
nothing for the triceps.
The triceps does

639
00:25:55,950 --> 00:25:58,050
nothing here, while the
biceps controls both

640
00:25:58,050 --> 00:26:01,030
the up and down
movement of the weight.

641
00:26:02,550 --> 00:26:05,610
How can it be that
the biceps brakii

642
00:26:05,610 --> 00:26:07,310
and other flexor
muscles of the elbow

643
00:26:07,310 --> 00:26:09,750
control both flexion
and extension

644
00:26:09,750 --> 00:26:12,790
and the triceps
brakii plays no role?

645
00:26:15,970 --> 00:26:18,250
In phase one of
this exercise,

646
00:26:18,250 --> 00:26:20,110
the biceps brakii
and other flexor

647
00:26:20,110 --> 00:26:21,750
muscles of the
elbow contract.

648
00:26:21,990 --> 00:26:24,690
The contraction is
sufficiently powerful to

649
00:26:24,690 --> 00:26:26,650
overcome the force of
gravity on the weight.

650
00:26:26,650 --> 00:26:28,990
The flexor muscles
shorten and the weight

651
00:26:28,990 --> 00:26:31,650
is lifted. This is a
concentric contraction.

652
00:26:32,970 --> 00:26:35,490
In phase two, the
weight must be

653
00:26:35,490 --> 00:26:37,990
lowered at a slow,
controlled rate. The

654
00:26:37,990 --> 00:26:40,410
gravitational pull
on the weight can

655
00:26:40,410 --> 00:26:42,390
extend the arm without
muscular input.

656
00:26:42,390 --> 00:26:44,370
The triceps is
therefore not needed

657
00:26:44,370 --> 00:26:46,370
to extend the arm
in this situation.

658
00:26:46,370 --> 00:26:47,630
As noted, the weight

659
00:26:47,630 --> 00:26:48,970
must be lowered slowly.

660
00:26:49,150 --> 00:26:51,330
Moreover, the
triceps can offer no

661
00:26:51,330 --> 00:26:53,350
assistance in slowing
the rate that the arm

662
00:26:53,350 --> 00:26:55,850
is extended since
it can only work in

663
00:26:55,850 --> 00:26:58,030
the same direction
as the weight itself.

664
00:26:58,150 --> 00:27:00,290
You might conclude
that the flexor

665
00:27:00,290 --> 00:27:03,090
muscles of the arm
must relax to lower

666
00:27:03,090 --> 00:27:05,470
the weight. But this
is not the case.

667
00:27:05,470 --> 00:27:07,570
If the flexor
muscles relaxed, the

668
00:27:07,570 --> 00:27:09,570
weight would fall
suddenly and rapidly.

669
00:27:09,610 --> 00:27:11,770
Again, the weight must
be lowered slowly.

670
00:27:11,830 --> 00:27:14,770
The flexor muscles must
continue to contract,

671
00:27:14,770 --> 00:27:16,830
but with a force
that is slightly less

672
00:27:16,830 --> 00:27:18,930
than the gravitational
pull of the weight.

673
00:27:18,930 --> 00:27:21,650
In this scenario,
the weight is slowly

674
00:27:21,650 --> 00:27:23,730
lowered and the
elbow is extended.

675
00:27:23,730 --> 00:27:25,470
due to the
controlled resistance

676
00:27:25,470 --> 00:27:26,990
of the flexor
muscles, which

677
00:27:26,990 --> 00:27:28,510
included the
biceps brachi.

678
00:27:29,250 --> 00:27:32,310
The muscle is
activated, but the

679
00:27:32,310 --> 00:27:34,730
weight is forcing
it to be elongated.

680
00:27:34,730 --> 00:27:36,710
This is an eccentric
contraction.

681
00:27:39,980 --> 00:27:41,890
This is easy to
understand if

682
00:27:41,890 --> 00:27:43,350
you compare it
to a tug-of-war.

683
00:27:43,350 --> 00:27:45,630
Muscles are
continuously in a tug

684
00:27:45,630 --> 00:27:47,890
-of-war battle with
opposing forces.

685
00:27:47,890 --> 00:27:50,670
Gravity is a constant
opposing force. In

686
00:27:50,670 --> 00:27:53,090
a tug-of-war battle
with gravity, in this

687
00:27:53,090 --> 00:27:55,490
scenario the muscle
overpowers gravity.

688
00:27:55,490 --> 00:27:57,750
The muscle shortens
and the muscle wins.

689
00:28:00,010 --> 00:28:02,610
During an eccentric
contraction, gravity

690
00:28:02,610 --> 00:28:05,130
and muscles are opposing
one another, but

691
00:28:05,130 --> 00:28:07,330
in this case gravity
is more powerful.

692
00:28:07,650 --> 00:28:09,510
Gravity pulls the
muscle with a force

693
00:28:09,510 --> 00:28:11,590
that exceeds the force
of the contraction.

694
00:28:11,850 --> 00:28:14,410
In some cases, the
force may simply

695
00:28:14,410 --> 00:28:15,790
be more powerful
than the full

696
00:28:15,790 --> 00:28:17,570
potential of the
muscle to resist.

697
00:28:17,830 --> 00:28:19,930
However, in most
circumstances, the

698
00:28:19,930 --> 00:28:22,570
muscle is allowing
gravity to win on purpose

699
00:28:22,570 --> 00:28:25,250
by exerting a
contractile force against

700
00:28:25,250 --> 00:28:27,130
gravity that
controls the rate of

701
00:28:27,130 --> 00:28:29,110
movement in the direction
that is opposite

702
00:28:29,110 --> 00:28:31,070
of the primary
action of the muscle.

703
00:28:31,110 --> 00:28:34,810
An eccentric contraction
always involves

704
00:28:34,810 --> 00:28:36,230
movement in the
direction that is

705
00:28:36,230 --> 00:28:38,290
in the opposite of
the primary action

706
00:28:38,290 --> 00:28:40,530
of the muscle. So,
for example, if

707
00:28:40,530 --> 00:28:43,370
a concentric contraction
of a particular

708
00:28:43,370 --> 00:28:45,630
muscle causes
flexion of a joint,

709
00:28:45,990 --> 00:28:48,470
An eccentric
contraction of the

710
00:28:48,470 --> 00:28:50,530
same muscle will
always involve

711
00:28:50,530 --> 00:28:52,410
extension of
the same joint.

712
00:28:54,610 --> 00:28:57,350
Because gravity is a
constant force, day

713
00:28:57,350 --> 00:28:59,150
-to-day movement of
the human body will

714
00:28:59,150 --> 00:29:03,470
always involve a complex
mixture of concentric

715
00:29:03,910 --> 00:29:06,670
and eccentric
contractions. As you study

716
00:29:06,670 --> 00:29:08,430
muscles and their
primary actions, it

717
00:29:08,430 --> 00:29:10,310
would be a good idea
to explore how use of

718
00:29:10,310 --> 00:29:12,850
those muscles might
involve concentric and

719
00:29:12,850 --> 00:29:15,060
eccentric contractions.
So, for instance,

720
00:29:15,340 --> 00:29:17,220
you might ask
yourself what exercise

721
00:29:17,220 --> 00:29:19,740
could one do that
involves both flexion and

722
00:29:19,740 --> 00:29:22,700
extension of the elbow
with contractions

723
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:25,320
of the biceps, of
the triceps brachi.

724
00:29:28,520 --> 00:29:31,540
So a push-up is a good
example of another

725
00:29:31,540 --> 00:29:35,400
simple exercise that
uses the triceps brachi

726
00:29:35,400 --> 00:29:37,660
that involves a
mixture of concentric

727
00:29:37,660 --> 00:29:39,860
and eccentric contractions
of the triceps

728
00:29:39,860 --> 00:29:42,100
brachi. On your own
time, consider the

729
00:29:42,100 --> 00:29:44,060
position of the elbow
during the up phase

730
00:29:44,060 --> 00:29:46,500
and down phase of a
push-up. What kinds

731
00:29:46,500 --> 00:29:48,480
of contractions of
the triceps brachii

732
00:29:48,480 --> 00:29:51,000
were involved to
reach each point? It's

733
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:52,680
easiest to remember
that an eccentric

734
00:29:52,680 --> 00:29:54,800
contraction is always
the opposite movement

735
00:29:54,800 --> 00:29:56,840
of the primary
action of the muscle.